    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old female named Shirley is receiving home hospice care for terminal pancreatic cancer. She develops severe, refractory bone pain and hyperactive delirium with agitation that cannot be safely or effectively managed at home despite multiple increases in transdermal and sublingual opioid doses. What is the most appropriate next step under the Medicare hospice benefit?', '["Transfer the patient to a General Inpatient (GIP) hospice bed for short-term, intensive management of acute, refractory symptoms that cannot be controlled in the home setting", "Disenroll the patient from hospice immediately and admit them to the hospital ICU for emergency intubation and chemotherapy", "Instruct the family to increase the opioid dose tenfold at home and use physical restraints to manage the delirium", "Refuse any change in care level, as home hospice patients are legally prohibited from leaving their residence", "Prescribe oral acetaminophen and advise the family that delirium is a normal part of dying that cannot be treated"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under the Medicare Hospice Benefit, **General Inpatient (GIP) care** is indicated for **short-term management of acute, refractory symptoms** (e.g., severe pain, uncontrollable delirium, intractable nausea, severe dyspnea) that **cannot be managed in other settings** (such as the home). GIP can be provided in an inpatient hospice unit, a skilled nursing facility, or a hospital. The focus remains entirely palliative, utilizing intensive nursing and medical adjustments to stabilize symptoms. Once the symptoms are controlled, the patient is transitioned back to home hospice care. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named Frank has been enrolled in hospice care for 8 months due to terminal metastatic colon cancer. He learns that he has been accepted into a new clinical trial evaluating a novel targeted immunotherapy that requires monthly hospital infusions and tumor biopsies. Which of the following is correct regarding his right to pursue this treatment?', '["If the patient revokes the hospice benefit, they face a permanent financial penalty and cannot re-enroll in hospice in the future", "Revoking hospice requires a court hearing and approval by a panel of three independent medical ethicists", "The patient can enroll in the clinical trial and receive chemotherapy while remaining on the hospice benefit, as hospice funds all experimental therapies", "Once enrolled in hospice, the patient is legally prohibited from revoking the benefit and must remain in hospice until death", "The patient has the absolute right to revoke the Medicare hospice benefit at any time to pursue curative or clinical trial therapies, returning to standard Medicare Part A and B coverage"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A patient (or their surrogate if the patient lacks capacity) has the **absolute right to revoke the election of the hospice benefit at any time**. Revocation immediately terminates the hospice benefit, and the patient **returns to standard Medicare coverage** (Parts A and B). This allows them to pursue curative treatments, clinical trials, or seek care from any provider. If the clinical trial is unsuccessful or the patient chooses to focus on comfort again, they **can re-enroll in hospice at any time**, provided they still meet the eligibility criteria (prognosis <= 6 months). (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
