    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old male named Lawrence has been enrolled in hospice care for 3 months due to terminal metastatic colon cancer. He learns that he has been accepted into a new clinical trial evaluating a novel targeted immunotherapy that requires monthly hospital infusions and tumor biopsies. Which of the following is correct regarding his right to pursue this treatment?', '["The patient has the absolute right to revoke the Medicare hospice benefit at any time to pursue curative or clinical trial therapies, returning to standard Medicare Part A and B coverage", "The patient can enroll in the clinical trial and receive chemotherapy while remaining on the hospice benefit, as hospice funds all experimental therapies", "If the patient revokes the hospice benefit, they face a permanent financial penalty and cannot re-enroll in hospice in the future", "Revoking hospice requires a court hearing and approval by a panel of three independent medical ethicists", "Once enrolled in hospice, the patient is legally prohibited from revoking the benefit and must remain in hospice until death"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A patient (or their surrogate if the patient lacks capacity) has the **absolute right to revoke the election of the hospice benefit at any time**. Revocation immediately terminates the hospice benefit, and the patient **returns to standard Medicare coverage** (Parts A and B). This allows them to pursue curative treatments, clinical trials, or seek care from any provider. If the clinical trial is unsuccessful or the patient chooses to focus on comfort again, they **can re-enroll in hospice at any time**, provided they still meet the eligibility criteria (prognosis <= 6 months). (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
