    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old female named Helen has been enrolled in hospice care for 6 months due to terminal metastatic colon cancer. She learns that she has been accepted into a new clinical trial evaluating a novel targeted immunotherapy that requires monthly hospital infusions and tumor biopsies. Which of the following is correct regarding her right to pursue this treatment?', '["The patient can enroll in the clinical trial and receive chemotherapy while remaining on the hospice benefit, as hospice funds all experimental therapies", "If the patient revokes the hospice benefit, they face a permanent financial penalty and cannot re-enroll in hospice in the future", "Revoking hospice requires a court hearing and approval by a panel of three independent medical ethicists", "Once enrolled in hospice, the patient is legally prohibited from revoking the benefit and must remain in hospice until death", "The patient has the absolute right to revoke the Medicare hospice benefit at any time to pursue curative or clinical trial therapies, returning to standard Medicare Part A and B coverage"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A patient (or their surrogate if the patient lacks capacity) has the **absolute right to revoke the election of the hospice benefit at any time**. Revocation immediately terminates the hospice benefit, and the patient **returns to standard Medicare coverage** (Parts A and B). This allows them to pursue curative treatments, clinical trials, or seek care from any provider. If the clinical trial is unsuccessful or the patient chooses to focus on comfort again, they **can re-enroll in hospice at any time**, provided they still meet the eligibility criteria (prognosis <= 6 months). (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female named Irene has been enrolled in hospice care for 3 months due to terminal metastatic colon cancer. She learns that she has been accepted into a new clinical trial evaluating a novel targeted immunotherapy that requires monthly hospital infusions and tumor biopsies. Which of the following is correct regarding her right to pursue this treatment?', '["If the patient revokes the hospice benefit, they face a permanent financial penalty and cannot re-enroll in hospice in the future", "The patient can enroll in the clinical trial and receive chemotherapy while remaining on the hospice benefit, as hospice funds all experimental therapies", "Once enrolled in hospice, the patient is legally prohibited from revoking the benefit and must remain in hospice until death", "Revoking hospice requires a court hearing and approval by a panel of three independent medical ethicists", "The patient has the absolute right to revoke the Medicare hospice benefit at any time to pursue curative or clinical trial therapies, returning to standard Medicare Part A and B coverage"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A patient (or their surrogate if the patient lacks capacity) has the **absolute right to revoke the election of the hospice benefit at any time**. Revocation immediately terminates the hospice benefit, and the patient **returns to standard Medicare coverage** (Parts A and B). This allows them to pursue curative treatments, clinical trials, or seek care from any provider. If the clinical trial is unsuccessful or the patient chooses to focus on comfort again, they **can re-enroll in hospice at any time**, provided they still meet the eligibility criteria (prognosis <= 6 months). (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female named Norma with advanced dementia is enrolled in hospice. Her family is struggling with emotional distress, spiritual questions, and practical challenges regarding daily care. Under the Medicare hospice benefit, how is the care delivery structured to address these multi-dimensional needs?', '["Hospice care is delivered exclusively by a single primary nurse, with no social work or spiritual support provided due to federal funding limits", "The patient''s family is required to hire and pay for their own independent social workers and chaplains, as these are not covered by hospice", "Hospice care is delivered by an Interdisciplinary Team (IDT) consisting of a hospice physician, nurse, social worker, chaplain, bereavement counselor, and trained volunteers, who collaborate to address physical, emotional, social, and spiritual needs", "Hospice care excludes all spiritual or emotional support, focusing strictly on the administration of intravenous pain medications", "The medical director makes all decisions in isolation and is prohibited by law from communicating with the nursing staff"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The core of the hospice model is the **Interdisciplinary Team (IDT)**. Federal regulations mandate that hospice care must be directed and coordinated by an IDT to address the physical, psychological, social, and spiritual needs of the patient and family. The IDT includes: (1) **Hospice Medical Director/Physician**, (2) **Registered Nurses**, (3) **Social Workers**, (4) **Chaplains/Spiritual Counselors**, (5) **Bereavement Counselors**, (6) **Hospice Aides**, and (7) **Trained Volunteers**. The team meets regularly to update a personalized plan of care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
