    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female named Ruby with end-stage congestive heart failure is evaluated for hospice eligibility. Which of the following clinical indicators best supports a prognosis of 6 months or less to qualify Ruby for the Medicare hospice benefit?', '["NYHA Class IV heart failure symptoms (dyspnea or fatigue at rest), optimal management with guideline-directed medical therapy, ejection fraction < 20% (if known), and recurrent hospitalizations or emergency department visits for heart failure exacerbations", "The presence of asymptomatic left bundle branch block on an electrocardiogram with normal echocardiography", "A history of a single myocardial infarction 10 years ago with normal current exercise tolerance and no current medications", "An ejection fraction of 45% with dyspnea occurring only during strenuous exercise (NYHA Class II)", "The patient''s refusal to take any medications, despite having asymptomatic hypertension and normal cardiac function"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For patients with non-cancer diagnoses like congestive heart failure, documenting a prognosis of <= 6 months can be challenging. Guidelines for hospice eligibility in **advanced heart failure** require: (1) **NYHA Class IV symptoms** (significant dyspnea, fatigue, or chest pain at rest or with minimal activity). (2) **Optimal medical management** (patient is treated with diuretics, ACEi/ARB/ARNI, beta-blockers, etc., or has documented intolerance). (3) **Clinical indicators of decline**, such as **recurrent hospitalizations or ED visits** for heart failure, cardiac cachexia, or refractory arrhythmias. An ejection fraction < 20% is supportive but not strictly mandatory. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 39 & 82)'),
