    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female named Gladys with end-stage congestive heart failure is evaluated for hospice eligibility. Which of the following clinical indicators best supports a prognosis of 6 months or less to qualify Gladys for the Medicare hospice benefit?', '["A history of a single myocardial infarction 10 years ago with normal current exercise tolerance and no current medications", "NYHA Class IV heart failure symptoms (dyspnea or fatigue at rest), optimal management with guideline-directed medical therapy, ejection fraction < 20% (if known), and recurrent hospitalizations or emergency department visits for heart failure exacerbations", "The patient''s refusal to take any medications, despite having asymptomatic hypertension and normal cardiac function", "The presence of asymptomatic left bundle branch block on an electrocardiogram with normal echocardiography", "An ejection fraction of 45% with dyspnea occurring only during strenuous exercise (NYHA Class II)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For patients with non-cancer diagnoses like congestive heart failure, documenting a prognosis of <= 6 months can be challenging. Guidelines for hospice eligibility in **advanced heart failure** require: (1) **NYHA Class IV symptoms** (significant dyspnea, fatigue, or chest pain at rest or with minimal activity). (2) **Optimal medical management** (patient is treated with diuretics, ACEi/ARB/ARNI, beta-blockers, etc., or has documented intolerance). (3) **Clinical indicators of decline**, such as **recurrent hospitalizations or ED visits** for heart failure, cardiac cachexia, or refractory arrhythmias. An ejection fraction < 20% is supportive but not strictly mandatory. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 39 & 82)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male named John with end-stage chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is being evaluated for hospice eligibility. Which of the following clinical findings is most indicative of a 6-month prognosis to support hospice enrollment?', '["A single episode of mild bronchitis that resolved completely with a 5-day course of oral azithromycin", "The presence of chronic cough without dyspnea, hypoxemia, or need for daily inhaler therapy", "A history of smoking 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 40 years, with normal current pulse oximetry on room air", "Severe, disabling dyspnea at rest, poor response to bronchodilators, oxygen dependence, and recurrent emergency department visits or hospitalizations for acute respiratory failure", "An FEV1 of 75% of predicted on spirometry with dyspnea only when climbing multiple flights of stairs"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hospice eligibility guidelines for **advanced COPD** focus on documenting severe lung dysfunction and progressive decline. Key indicators include: (1) **Severe, disabling dyspnea at rest** or with minimal exertion, with little to no response to bronchodilators. (2) **Oxygen dependence** (chronic hypoxemia at rest, pO2 <= 55 mmHg or O2 saturation <= 88% on supplemental oxygen). (3) **FEV1 <= 30% of predicted** (if spirometry is available, though not required). (4) **Progressive decline**, shown by **recurrent hospitalizations or emergency visits** for respiratory failure or pulmonary infections, weight loss, or cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure). (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
