    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female named Lillian with stage G4 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 22 mL/min) and metastatic prostate cancer is experiencing severe, constant bone pain. Which of the following opioids should be **avoided** due to the risk of metabolite accumulation and neurotoxicity, and which is the **preferred** alternative for long-term pain management?', '["Avoid transdermal buprenorphine (due to high renal clearance); prefer oral meperidine", "Avoid methadone (because it is highly dialyzable); prefer oral codeine", "Avoid fentanyl (due to direct renal tubular necrosis); prefer morphine or codeine", "Avoid morphine (due to accumulation of active metabolites morphine-3-glucuronide and morphine-6-glucuronide, causing myoclonus, seizures, and respiratory depression); prefer fentanyl or hydromorphone", "Avoid hydromorphone (due to its lack of analgesic effect in renal failure); prefer codeine"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In patients with **renal impairment (CKD stage G4/G5)**, several opioids are hazardous. **Morphine and codeine must be avoided**. Morphine is metabolized to **morphine-3-glucuronide (M3G)** (which causes neurotoxicity: myoclonus, hyperalgesia, delirium, seizures) and **morphine-6-glucuronide (M6G)** (a potent analgesic that accumulates, causing profound respiratory depression and sedation). Codeine also accumulates. **Preferred opioids in renal failure are fentanyl, hydromorphone, and methadone**. Fentanyl is metabolized to inactive metabolites and is not cleared renally. Hydromorphone has metabolites (H3G) but is safer than morphine when used cautiously. Methadone has primarily fecal excretion. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
