    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old male named David with stage G4 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 22 mL/min) and metastatic prostate cancer is experiencing severe, constant bone pain. Which of the following opioids should be **avoided** due to the risk of metabolite accumulation and neurotoxicity, and which is the **preferred** alternative for long-term pain management?', '["Avoid methadone (because it is highly dialyzable); prefer oral codeine", "Avoid hydromorphone (due to its lack of analgesic effect in renal failure); prefer codeine", "Avoid transdermal buprenorphine (due to high renal clearance); prefer oral meperidine", "Avoid fentanyl (due to direct renal tubular necrosis); prefer morphine or codeine", "Avoid morphine (due to accumulation of active metabolites morphine-3-glucuronide and morphine-6-glucuronide, causing myoclonus, seizures, and respiratory depression); prefer fentanyl or hydromorphone"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In patients with **renal impairment (CKD stage G4/G5)**, several opioids are hazardous. **Morphine and codeine must be avoided**. Morphine is metabolized to **morphine-3-glucuronide (M3G)** (which causes neurotoxicity: myoclonus, hyperalgesia, delirium, seizures) and **morphine-6-glucuronide (M6G)** (a potent analgesic that accumulates, causing profound respiratory depression and sedation). Codeine also accumulates. **Preferred opioids in renal failure are fentanyl, hydromorphone, and methadone**. Fentanyl is metabolized to inactive metabolites and is not cleared renally. Hydromorphone has metabolites (H3G) but is safer than morphine when used cautiously. Methadone has primarily fecal excretion. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female named Frances with stage G4 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 22 mL/min) and metastatic prostate cancer is experiencing severe, constant bone pain. Which of the following opioids should be **avoided** due to the risk of metabolite accumulation and neurotoxicity, and which is the **preferred** alternative for long-term pain management?', '["Avoid fentanyl (due to direct renal tubular necrosis); prefer morphine or codeine", "Avoid hydromorphone (due to its lack of analgesic effect in renal failure); prefer codeine", "Avoid methadone (because it is highly dialyzable); prefer oral codeine", "Avoid transdermal buprenorphine (due to high renal clearance); prefer oral meperidine", "Avoid morphine (due to accumulation of active metabolites morphine-3-glucuronide and morphine-6-glucuronide, causing myoclonus, seizures, and respiratory depression); prefer fentanyl or hydromorphone"]'::jsonb, 4, 'In patients with **renal impairment (CKD stage G4/G5)**, several opioids are hazardous. **Morphine and codeine must be avoided**. Morphine is metabolized to **morphine-3-glucuronide (M3G)** (which causes neurotoxicity: myoclonus, hyperalgesia, delirium, seizures) and **morphine-6-glucuronide (M6G)** (a potent analgesic that accumulates, causing profound respiratory depression and sedation). Codeine also accumulates. **Preferred opioids in renal failure are fentanyl, hydromorphone, and methadone**. Fentanyl is metabolized to inactive metabolites and is not cleared renally. Hydromorphone has metabolites (H3G) but is safer than morphine when used cautiously. Methadone has primarily fecal excretion. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old male named Frank has been taking oral morphine 60 mg daily for metastatic bone pain. He has developed severe, intractable myoclonus and sedation, prompting the clinician to rotate his opioid to oral oxycodone. The equianalgesic ratio is: **30 mg oral morphine = 20 mg oral oxycodone**. Which of the following represents the most appropriate clinical reasoning and step in calculating the new oxycodone dose?', '["Calculate the equianalgesic oxycodone dose (which is 40 mg/day), and then reduce this dose by 25-50% to account for incomplete cross-tolerance, resulting in a starting dose of approximately 28 mg/day split into scheduled doses", "Prescribe oxycodone on an as-needed basis only, discontinuing all scheduled baseline pain medications", "Prescribe the exact equianalgesic dose of oxycodone (40 mg/day) without any reduction, as cross-tolerance is always complete", "Switch to oral acetaminophen 10 g daily, as opioids cannot be rotated safely in older adults", "Double the calculated equianalgesic dose of oxycodone to prevent a withdrawal syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When rotating opioids due to side effects (like myoclonus from morphine) or poor pain control, clinicians must adjust for **incomplete cross-tolerance**. Because receptors are not identical across different opioids, a patient tolerant to one opioid will be highly sensitive to a new one. The step-by-step method is: (1) Calculate the 24-hour total dose of the current opioid. (2) Convert to the new opioid using equianalgesic tables. (3) **Reduce the calculated equianalgesic dose of the new opioid by 25% to 50%** to prevent overdose/respiratory depression. (4) Divide into scheduled doses and provide 10-15% of the daily dose as a short-acting PRN opioid for breakthrough pain. (Ref: Palliative Care: A Practical Guide Ch. 6; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female named Alice has been taking oral morphine 90 mg daily for metastatic bone pain. She has developed severe, intractable myoclonus and sedation, prompting the clinician to rotate her opioid to oral oxycodone. The equianalgesic ratio is: **30 mg oral morphine = 20 mg oral oxycodone**. Which of the following represents the most appropriate clinical reasoning and step in calculating the new oxycodone dose?', '["Prescribe oxycodone on an as-needed basis only, discontinuing all scheduled baseline pain medications", "Calculate the equianalgesic oxycodone dose (which is 60 mg/day), and then reduce this dose by 25-50% to account for incomplete cross-tolerance, resulting in a starting dose of approximately 42 mg/day split into scheduled doses", "Prescribe the exact equianalgesic dose of oxycodone (60 mg/day) without any reduction, as cross-tolerance is always complete", "Switch to oral acetaminophen 10 g daily, as opioids cannot be rotated safely in older adults", "Double the calculated equianalgesic dose of oxycodone to prevent a withdrawal syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When rotating opioids due to side effects (like myoclonus from morphine) or poor pain control, clinicians must adjust for **incomplete cross-tolerance**. Because receptors are not identical across different opioids, a patient tolerant to one opioid will be highly sensitive to a new one. The step-by-step method is: (1) Calculate the 24-hour total dose of the current opioid. (2) Convert to the new opioid using equianalgesic tables. (3) **Reduce the calculated equianalgesic dose of the new opioid by 25% to 50%** to prevent overdose/respiratory depression. (4) Divide into scheduled doses and provide 10-15% of the daily dose as a short-acting PRN opioid for breakthrough pain. (Ref: Palliative Care: A Practical Guide Ch. 6; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Margaret has been taking oral morphine 150 mg daily for metastatic bone pain. She has developed severe, intractable myoclonus and sedation, prompting the clinician to rotate her opioid to oral oxycodone. The equianalgesic ratio is: **30 mg oral morphine = 20 mg oral oxycodone**. Which of the following represents the most appropriate clinical reasoning and step in calculating the new oxycodone dose?', '["Prescribe the exact equianalgesic dose of oxycodone (100 mg/day) without any reduction, as cross-tolerance is always complete", "Calculate the equianalgesic oxycodone dose (which is 100 mg/day), and then reduce this dose by 25-50% to account for incomplete cross-tolerance, resulting in a starting dose of approximately 70 mg/day split into scheduled doses", "Prescribe oxycodone on an as-needed basis only, discontinuing all scheduled baseline pain medications", "Double the calculated equianalgesic dose of oxycodone to prevent a withdrawal syndrome", "Switch to oral acetaminophen 10 g daily, as opioids cannot be rotated safely in older adults"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When rotating opioids due to side effects (like myoclonus from morphine) or poor pain control, clinicians must adjust for **incomplete cross-tolerance**. Because receptors are not identical across different opioids, a patient tolerant to one opioid will be highly sensitive to a new one. The step-by-step method is: (1) Calculate the 24-hour total dose of the current opioid. (2) Convert to the new opioid using equianalgesic tables. (3) **Reduce the calculated equianalgesic dose of the new opioid by 25% to 50%** to prevent overdose/respiratory depression. (4) Divide into scheduled doses and provide 10-15% of the daily dose as a short-acting PRN opioid for breakthrough pain. (Ref: Palliative Care: A Practical Guide Ch. 6; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
