    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old male named Arthur has been taking oral morphine 90 mg daily for metastatic bone pain. He has developed severe, intractable myoclonus and sedation, prompting the clinician to rotate his opioid to oral oxycodone. The equianalgesic ratio is: **30 mg oral morphine = 20 mg oral oxycodone**. Which of the following represents the most appropriate clinical reasoning and step in calculating the new oxycodone dose?', '["Switch to oral acetaminophen 10 g daily, as opioids cannot be rotated safely in older adults", "Calculate the equianalgesic oxycodone dose (which is 60 mg/day), and then reduce this dose by 25-50% to account for incomplete cross-tolerance, resulting in a starting dose of approximately 42 mg/day split into scheduled doses", "Prescribe oxycodone on an as-needed basis only, discontinuing all scheduled baseline pain medications", "Prescribe the exact equianalgesic dose of oxycodone (60 mg/day) without any reduction, as cross-tolerance is always complete", "Double the calculated equianalgesic dose of oxycodone to prevent a withdrawal syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When rotating opioids due to side effects (like myoclonus from morphine) or poor pain control, clinicians must adjust for **incomplete cross-tolerance**. Because receptors are not identical across different opioids, a patient tolerant to one opioid will be highly sensitive to a new one. The step-by-step method is: (1) Calculate the 24-hour total dose of the current opioid. (2) Convert to the new opioid using equianalgesic tables. (3) **Reduce the calculated equianalgesic dose of the new opioid by 25% to 50%** to prevent overdose/respiratory depression. (4) Divide into scheduled doses and provide 10-15% of the daily dose as a short-acting PRN opioid for breakthrough pain. (Ref: Palliative Care: A Practical Guide Ch. 6; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old male named Paul has been taking oral morphine 90 mg daily for metastatic bone pain. He has developed severe, intractable myoclonus and sedation, prompting the clinician to rotate his opioid to oral oxycodone. The equianalgesic ratio is: **30 mg oral morphine = 20 mg oral oxycodone**. Which of the following represents the most appropriate clinical reasoning and step in calculating the new oxycodone dose?', '["Prescribe oxycodone on an as-needed basis only, discontinuing all scheduled baseline pain medications", "Calculate the equianalgesic oxycodone dose (which is 60 mg/day), and then reduce this dose by 25-50% to account for incomplete cross-tolerance, resulting in a starting dose of approximately 42 mg/day split into scheduled doses", "Switch to oral acetaminophen 10 g daily, as opioids cannot be rotated safely in older adults", "Double the calculated equianalgesic dose of oxycodone to prevent a withdrawal syndrome", "Prescribe the exact equianalgesic dose of oxycodone (60 mg/day) without any reduction, as cross-tolerance is always complete"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When rotating opioids due to side effects (like myoclonus from morphine) or poor pain control, clinicians must adjust for **incomplete cross-tolerance**. Because receptors are not identical across different opioids, a patient tolerant to one opioid will be highly sensitive to a new one. The step-by-step method is: (1) Calculate the 24-hour total dose of the current opioid. (2) Convert to the new opioid using equianalgesic tables. (3) **Reduce the calculated equianalgesic dose of the new opioid by 25% to 50%** to prevent overdose/respiratory depression. (4) Divide into scheduled doses and provide 10-15% of the daily dose as a short-acting PRN opioid for breakthrough pain. (Ref: Palliative Care: A Practical Guide Ch. 6; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Rose has been taking oral morphine 150 mg daily for metastatic bone pain. She has developed severe, intractable myoclonus and sedation, prompting the clinician to rotate her opioid to oral oxycodone. The equianalgesic ratio is: **30 mg oral morphine = 20 mg oral oxycodone**. Which of the following represents the most appropriate clinical reasoning and step in calculating the new oxycodone dose?', '["Double the calculated equianalgesic dose of oxycodone to prevent a withdrawal syndrome", "Switch to oral acetaminophen 10 g daily, as opioids cannot be rotated safely in older adults", "Prescribe the exact equianalgesic dose of oxycodone (100 mg/day) without any reduction, as cross-tolerance is always complete", "Prescribe oxycodone on an as-needed basis only, discontinuing all scheduled baseline pain medications", "Calculate the equianalgesic oxycodone dose (which is 100 mg/day), and then reduce this dose by 25-50% to account for incomplete cross-tolerance, resulting in a starting dose of approximately 70 mg/day split into scheduled doses"]'::jsonb, 4, 'When rotating opioids due to side effects (like myoclonus from morphine) or poor pain control, clinicians must adjust for **incomplete cross-tolerance**. Because receptors are not identical across different opioids, a patient tolerant to one opioid will be highly sensitive to a new one. The step-by-step method is: (1) Calculate the 24-hour total dose of the current opioid. (2) Convert to the new opioid using equianalgesic tables. (3) **Reduce the calculated equianalgesic dose of the new opioid by 25% to 50%** to prevent overdose/respiratory depression. (4) Divide into scheduled doses and provide 10-15% of the daily dose as a short-acting PRN opioid for breakthrough pain. (Ref: Palliative Care: A Practical Guide Ch. 6; Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old male named Albert with advanced lung cancer is managed for constant chest wall pain with a long-acting transdermal fentanyl patch at a dose of 50 mcg/hour (equivalent to approximately 100 mg of oral morphine daily). He reports experiencing sudden, severe pain spikes (breakthrough pain) 3 to 4 times a day, particularly when coughing. What is the most appropriate breakthrough pain regimen to prescribe?', '["Prescribe short-acting oral immediate-release morphine (approx. 10 mg, which represents 10-15% of the total 24-hour baseline morphine-equivalent dose) to be taken every 4 hours as needed for breakthrough pain", "Increase the fentanyl patch dose to 400 mcg/hour immediately to eliminate the need for any breakthrough medications", "Prescribe long-acting oral methadone to be taken every 4 hours as needed for breakthrough pain", "Instruct the patient to apply a second fentanyl patch only during the episodes of coughing and remove it after 30 minutes", "Recommend taking over-the-counter ibuprofen 800 mg every 2 hours, as opioids should not be used for breakthrough pain"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For patients on a stable, long-acting opioid regimen (like transdermal fentanyl or extended-release morphine) who experience **breakthrough pain**, the standard of care is to prescribe a **short-acting, immediate-release opioid** (e.g., immediate-release morphine, oxycodone, or hydromorphone). The breakthrough dose is calculated as **10% to 15% of the total 24-hour baseline oral morphine-equivalent (OME) dose**, administered on an **as-needed (PRN)** basis. For example, if a patient is on 100 mg of OME per day, the breakthrough dose should be 10-15 mg of immediate-release morphine PRN. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female named Lillian with advanced lung cancer is managed for constant chest wall pain with a long-acting transdermal fentanyl patch at a dose of 50 mcg/hour (equivalent to approximately 100 mg of oral morphine daily). She reports experiencing sudden, severe pain spikes (breakthrough pain) 3 to 4 times a day, particularly when coughing. What is the most appropriate breakthrough pain regimen to prescribe?', '["Recommend taking over-the-counter ibuprofen 800 mg every 2 hours, as opioids should not be used for breakthrough pain", "Prescribe short-acting oral immediate-release morphine (approx. 10 mg, which represents 10-15% of the total 24-hour baseline morphine-equivalent dose) to be taken every 4 hours as needed for breakthrough pain", "Increase the fentanyl patch dose to 400 mcg/hour immediately to eliminate the need for any breakthrough medications", "Instruct the patient to apply a second fentanyl patch only during the episodes of coughing and remove it after 30 minutes", "Prescribe long-acting oral methadone to be taken every 4 hours as needed for breakthrough pain"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For patients on a stable, long-acting opioid regimen (like transdermal fentanyl or extended-release morphine) who experience **breakthrough pain**, the standard of care is to prescribe a **short-acting, immediate-release opioid** (e.g., immediate-release morphine, oxycodone, or hydromorphone). The breakthrough dose is calculated as **10% to 15% of the total 24-hour baseline oral morphine-equivalent (OME) dose**, administered on an **as-needed (PRN)** basis. For example, if a patient is on 100 mg of OME per day, the breakthrough dose should be 10-15 mg of immediate-release morphine PRN. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Marie with advanced lung cancer is managed for constant chest wall pain with a long-acting transdermal fentanyl patch at a dose of 100 mcg/hour (equivalent to approximately 200 mg of oral morphine daily). She reports experiencing sudden, severe pain spikes (breakthrough pain) 3 to 4 times a day, particularly when coughing. What is the most appropriate breakthrough pain regimen to prescribe?', '["Prescribe short-acting oral immediate-release morphine (approx. 20 mg, which represents 10-15% of the total 24-hour baseline morphine-equivalent dose) to be taken every 4 hours as needed for breakthrough pain", "Recommend taking over-the-counter ibuprofen 800 mg every 2 hours, as opioids should not be used for breakthrough pain", "Increase the fentanyl patch dose to 400 mcg/hour immediately to eliminate the need for any breakthrough medications", "Prescribe long-acting oral methadone to be taken every 4 hours as needed for breakthrough pain", "Instruct the patient to apply a second fentanyl patch only during the episodes of coughing and remove it after 30 minutes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For patients on a stable, long-acting opioid regimen (like transdermal fentanyl or extended-release morphine) who experience **breakthrough pain**, the standard of care is to prescribe a **short-acting, immediate-release opioid** (e.g., immediate-release morphine, oxycodone, or hydromorphone). The breakthrough dose is calculated as **10% to 15% of the total 24-hour baseline oral morphine-equivalent (OME) dose**, administered on an **as-needed (PRN)** basis. For example, if a patient is on 100 mg of OME per day, the breakthrough dose should be 10-15 mg of immediate-release morphine PRN. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
