    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female named Gladys with advanced lung cancer is managed for constant chest wall pain with a long-acting transdermal fentanyl patch at a dose of 75 mcg/hour (equivalent to approximately 150 mg of oral morphine daily). She reports experiencing sudden, severe pain spikes (breakthrough pain) 3 to 4 times a day, particularly when coughing. What is the most appropriate breakthrough pain regimen to prescribe?', '["Instruct the patient to apply a second fentanyl patch only during the episodes of coughing and remove it after 30 minutes", "Increase the fentanyl patch dose to 400 mcg/hour immediately to eliminate the need for any breakthrough medications", "Prescribe long-acting oral methadone to be taken every 4 hours as needed for breakthrough pain", "Prescribe short-acting oral immediate-release morphine (approx. 15 mg, which represents 10-15% of the total 24-hour baseline morphine-equivalent dose) to be taken every 4 hours as needed for breakthrough pain", "Recommend taking over-the-counter ibuprofen 800 mg every 2 hours, as opioids should not be used for breakthrough pain"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For patients on a stable, long-acting opioid regimen (like transdermal fentanyl or extended-release morphine) who experience **breakthrough pain**, the standard of care is to prescribe a **short-acting, immediate-release opioid** (e.g., immediate-release morphine, oxycodone, or hydromorphone). The breakthrough dose is calculated as **10% to 15% of the total 24-hour baseline oral morphine-equivalent (OME) dose**, administered on an **as-needed (PRN)** basis. For example, if a patient is on 100 mg of OME per day, the breakthrough dose should be 10-15 mg of immediate-release morphine PRN. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old male named Roy with metastatic breast cancer is started on scheduled oral morphine for pain control. Which of the following best describes the management of opioid-induced constipation (OIC) in this patient?', '["Prescribe oral docusate sodium monotherapy, as surfactant stool softeners are highly effective in fully preventing OIC", "Advise the patient to significantly increase dietary fiber to 60 g daily, which is sufficient to overcome OIC without laxatives", "Recommend a ''wait-and-see'' approach, initiating laxatives only if the patient has not had a bowel movement for 10 days", "Initiate a prophylactic stimulant laxative (e.g., bisacodyl) combined with an osmotic laxative (e.g., polyethylene glycol); explain that tolerance to opioid-induced constipation does not develop, so prophylaxis is required for the duration of opioid therapy", "Explain that tolerance to constipation develops within 2 weeks of starting opioids, so laxatives are only needed temporarily"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Opioid-induced constipation (OIC)** is a universal side effect caused by opioids binding to mu-receptors in the enteric nervous system, reducing peristalsis and secretions. Unlike other side effects (nausea, sedation), **tolerance to constipation does not develop**. Therefore, **prophylactic laxatives must be started concurrently** with opioid initiation and continued throughout therapy. The preferred regimen is a **stimulant laxative (senna or bisacodyl)** to stimulate peristalsis, often paired with an **osmotic laxative (polyethylene glycol)**. Docusate (stool softener) alone is ineffective. For refractory OIC, peripheral mu-opioid receptor antagonists (PAMORAs, e.g., methylnaltrexone) are indicated. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
