    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female named Gertrude with metastatic breast cancer is started on scheduled oral morphine for pain control. Which of the following best describes the management of opioid-induced constipation (OIC) in this patient?', '["Advise the patient to significantly increase dietary fiber to 60 g daily, which is sufficient to overcome OIC without laxatives", "Explain that tolerance to constipation develops within 2 weeks of starting opioids, so laxatives are only needed temporarily", "Prescribe oral docusate sodium monotherapy, as surfactant stool softeners are highly effective in fully preventing OIC", "Initiate a prophylactic stimulant laxative (e.g., bisacodyl) combined with an osmotic laxative (e.g., polyethylene glycol); explain that tolerance to opioid-induced constipation does not develop, so prophylaxis is required for the duration of opioid therapy", "Recommend a ''wait-and-see'' approach, initiating laxatives only if the patient has not had a bowel movement for 10 days"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Opioid-induced constipation (OIC)** is a universal side effect caused by opioids binding to mu-receptors in the enteric nervous system, reducing peristalsis and secretions. Unlike other side effects (nausea, sedation), **tolerance to constipation does not develop**. Therefore, **prophylactic laxatives must be started concurrently** with opioid initiation and continued throughout therapy. The preferred regimen is a **stimulant laxative (senna or bisacodyl)** to stimulate peristalsis, often paired with an **osmotic laxative (polyethylene glycol)**. Docusate (stool softener) alone is ineffective. For refractory OIC, peripheral mu-opioid receptor antagonists (PAMORAs, e.g., methylnaltrexone) are indicated. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
