    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female named Florence with advanced renal failure is receiving high-dose oral morphine for cancer pain. Over the past 48 hours, she has become increasingly agitated and confused. Physical examination reveals diffuse myoclonus (muscle jerking) and severe, generalized pain triggered even by light touch (allodynia). What is the underlying pathophysiology of these symptoms, and what is the most appropriate management?', '["Severe progression of the cancer metastases requiring an immediate 50% increase in the morphine dose", "Acetaminophen toxicity; administer intravenous N-acetylcysteine immediately", "Opioid-induced neurotoxicity (OIN) due to accumulation of the morphine metabolite morphine-3-glucuronide (M3G); manage by rotating to a different opioid (e.g., fentanyl or methadone) and ensuring gentle hydration", "Acute ischemic stroke requiring emergent administration of intravenous tissue plasminogen activator (tPA)", "A normal age-related change in pain perception that should be managed with physical therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Opioid-induced neurotoxicity (OIN)** is a syndrome characterized by cognitive impairment, delirium, agitation, myoclonus, seizures, and **hyperalgesia/allodynia** (paradoxical worsening of pain). It is primarily caused by the accumulation of active metabolites, particularly **morphine-3-glucuronide (M3G)**, which accumulates in patients with **renal impairment**. Hyperalgesia is often mistaken for cancer pain progression, leading to dangerous dose increases that worsen the neurotoxicity. Management includes: (1) **Opioid rotation** to an alternative agent (e.g., fentanyl, methadone), (2) Dose reduction, (3) **Hydration** to facilitate renal clearance of metabolites. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
